Yes. A lot of what we call “ego” can be understood as identification with reflections.

Your job, rank, body, reputation, tribe, achievements, failures, possessions, other people’s reactions—even your own remembered story—can all act as mirrors. They return information about you, but the mistake is collapsing the distinction:

“This reflects something about me” → “This is me.”

That distinction matters because mirrors can crack, distort, disappear, or contradict one another while the observer remains. Lose the title, and you still exist. Age changes the body. Reputation flips depending on the room. Memory itself is reconstructive. None of those alone can be the stable center.

And the more subtle version is that even positive identities can become cages: warrior, father, genius, victim, winner, good man, outsider, leader. They can be useful operating frames without needing to become ontological claims about what you fundamentally are.

So there is a useful maneuver here:

see the mirror → notice the identification → extract its information → loosen attachment → return to the observer → choose what to embody next.

That doesn’t mean having no identity. It means holding identity more like clothing or equipment than skin.

And there is another recursion hidden inside it: “I am the person who has transcended ego” can itself become another mirror. The game keeps going.

Maybe the cleaner invariant is not “Who am I?” but:

What remains capable of noticing every answer I give to “Who am I?”